<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<assembly xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
          xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/plugins/maven-assembly-plugin/assembly/1.1.2"
          xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/plugins/maven-assembly-plugin/assembly/1.1.2 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/assembly-1.1.2.xsd">
    <!-- Slim, single-platform variant of jar-with-dependencies for linux-aarch_64.

         This is ADDITIVE: it is attached alongside (never in place of) the default
         mockserver-netty-<version>-jar-with-dependencies.jar, which stays byte-for-byte
         unchanged. Consumers that share one Maven/Gradle configuration across mixed
         developer laptops (macOS/Windows) and Linux CI must keep using the default all-
         platforms jar; this classified artifact is opt-in for the empty-disk case
         (containers, cold CI agents) where ~18 MiB of foreign-platform natives is pure
         download cost.

         The fat jar bundles netty native binaries for EVERY platform under
         META-INF/native/: linux, macOS and windows, each in x86_64 + aarch_64. A
         linux-aarch_64 process can only load its own arch's ELF .so - the macOS blobs are
         Mach-O (.jnilib) and the Windows ones are PE (.dll), object formats an ELF loader
         physically cannot map, and the wrong-arch linux .so is just as unusable.

         FILTER SHAPE - this matches docker/Dockerfile's `jarprep` stage exactly. The KEEP
         rule is the "*<arch>.so" SUFFIX, expressed here as: exclude the whole
         META-INF/native/ tree in the two unpack sets, then add back ONLY this arch's
         "*aarch_64.so" in the third set. The suffix form is deliberate and must not be
         narrowed to a "linux_<arch>" match: this arch's linux natives are THREE
         differently-named files -
             libnetty_tcnative_linux_aarch_64.so
             libnetty_quiche42_linux_aarch_64.so
             libnetty_transport_native_epoll_aarch_64.so   <- note: NO "linux_" in the name
         so a "linux_<arch>" filter would silently drop the epoll .so and degrade epoll to
         NIO with no error. The "*aarch_64.so" suffix keeps all three ELF .so and drops
         every .jnilib / .dll / wrong-arch entry (the osx "*_aarch_64.jnilib" contain the
         arch string but are Mach-O, and are correctly excluded because the third set only
         adds back ".so"). NEVER narrow this so it could remove this arch's epoll, tcnative
         or quiche .so. -->
    <id>jar-with-dependencies-linux-aarch_64</id>
    <formats>
        <format>jar</format>
    </formats>
    <includeBaseDirectory>false</includeBaseDirectory>
    <dependencySets>
        <!-- all non-native content (identical to the default jar), provided scope -->
        <dependencySet>
            <outputDirectory>/</outputDirectory>
            <useProjectArtifact>true</useProjectArtifact>
            <unpack>true</unpack>
            <scope>provided</scope>
            <unpackOptions>
                <excludes>
                    <exclude>META-INF/native/**</exclude>
                </excludes>
            </unpackOptions>
        </dependencySet>
        <!-- all non-native content (identical to the default jar), runtime scope -->
        <dependencySet>
            <outputDirectory>/</outputDirectory>
            <useProjectArtifact>true</useProjectArtifact>
            <unpack>true</unpack>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
            <unpackOptions>
                <excludes>
                    <exclude>META-INF/native/**</exclude>
                </excludes>
            </unpackOptions>
        </dependencySet>
        <!-- add back ONLY this platform's linux ELF natives (suffix keep, see header) -->
        <dependencySet>
            <outputDirectory>/</outputDirectory>
            <useProjectArtifact>true</useProjectArtifact>
            <unpack>true</unpack>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
            <unpackOptions>
                <includes>
                    <include>META-INF/native/*aarch_64.so</include>
                </includes>
            </unpackOptions>
        </dependencySet>
    </dependencySets>
</assembly>
